00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.840
Celebrity is as old as NFTs are new.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.830
Some consider Alexander the
Great the first celebrity ever

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.470
thanks to his power, riches,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.230
and face pretty enough to slap on a coin.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.330
But, celebrity culture
as we know it today,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.610
as scholar Thomas Mole argues,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.900
really got going in the Romantic era,

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.200
when certain authors started
cultivating public personality,

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.500
something we might now
call personal brands.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:06.990
Like Lord Byron,

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.570
who became famous not
just for his dank poems,

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.830
but also for the way he
dressed and the way he behaved,

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:16.340
including being a bit of
a dirtbag with the ladies.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.000
The 19th century brought the
emergence of photography,

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.010
which changed everything.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.210
Indeed, Abraham Lincoln
personally credited a photographer

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.450
of a widely circulated image of him

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.870
as a chief reason he
was elected president.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.110
While painted portraiture
had long been a thing

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.370
for the rich and famous,

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:33.900
photography newly allowed

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.440
for the mass dissemination of images,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.590
often images of humans,
especially famous humans.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.930
With each subsequent
technology that followed,

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.943
images and then moving images
of celebrity became more

00:05:45.943 --> 00:05:48.310
and more accessible until we wind up

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.450
with Cristiano Ronaldo setting the record

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.530
for the most Instagram followers ever.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.210
And, the more images of
celebrities circulated,

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.270
the bigger and broader celebrity became

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.660
and this makes sense.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.810
After all, celebrity is all
about being visible, being seen.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.120
In this sense, celebrities can cynically

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.380
be considered commodities
that are consumed,

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.360
ie, looked at by the masses and,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.160
as scholar Pramod Nayar points out

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.410
in his book "Seeing Stars,"

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.890
there's not really a meaningful
difference between, say,

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:21.330
a promotional campaign for Diet Coke

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:22.670
and a promotional campaign

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.060
for a living, breathing celebrity.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:26.927
After all, "Celebrities," he says,

00:06:26.927 --> 00:06:29.520
"are materials to be branded and sold.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.840
Fame is a manufactured product,

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.120
manufactured in and by a mass
media for public consumption."

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.130
A tabloid magazine, for instance,

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.600
may be its own standalone commodity.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.500
But, it's predicated on
even more commodities:

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.710
The celebrities being reported on.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.910
Because celebrities are
essentially commodities themselves,

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.300
perhaps it's no surprise
that they've long been seen

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.680
as essential to creating and
sustaining the demand necessary

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.080
for the consumer economy.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.137
As scholar P. David Marshall puts it,

00:06:59.137 --> 00:07:01.570
"Celebrity taught
generations how to engage

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:05.030
and use consumer culture to make oneself."

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:07.730
Ie, celebrity and
consumerism were a match made

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.421
in capitalist heaven, which
is also known as regular hell.

00:07:11.421 --> 00:07:12.254
(man screams)

00:07:12.254 --> 00:07:14.560
This was happening back
in those Romantic years

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.510
when people bought ruffley
shirts to make themselves look

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:20.239
a touch more like Lord
Byron, for instance.

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:22.030
But, that was nothing compared

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.960
to how the inextricable
relationship between celebrity

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.650
and consumer culture has grown
in the years that followed.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.580
Magazines from the 1940s
reported breathlessly

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:33.960
on how stars spend their fortune,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.570
showcasing their glamorous homes

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.670
and enviable spending habits.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.380
First Lady Jackie Kennedy's
fondness for Chanel suits,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.860
among other sleek designs,

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.127
practically defined sixties fashion.

00:07:44.127 --> 00:07:46.670
"MTV Cribs" showed us
that the ultimate ideal

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:48.460
of a dope ass house might have

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:52.240
its own dedicated lingerie
closet or 35 bathrooms.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.900
Basically, it's a version of monkey see,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.030
monkey really wants to do.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.060
But, instead of foraging for bananas,

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.010
we're reaching for new
markers of class and taste.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.640
- I saw Cady Heron wearing
army pants and flip flops.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.120
So I bought army pants and flip flops.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.120
- And nowhere is the relationship
between consumer culture

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:14.290
and celebrity more obvious than
with celebrity endorsements.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.950
So, why does celebrity
endorsement sell everything

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.260
from cars to Coors Light so well?

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.980
Nayer argues that it's
because of the phenomenon

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:27.080
of transferred celebrity, ie,
the product becomes associated

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.840
with the qualities of the human celebrity.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.650
So, let's say it's 2004.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.540
You love Britney Spears and
you want everyone to know it

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.900
and to also associate
you with how cool she is.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.120
By buying a pair of the
Sketchers she recently endorsed

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:41.740
in the pages of "Teen People,"

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.198
you're trying to imbue
yourself with as much

00:08:44.198 --> 00:08:47.350
of Britney's bubblegum charm, as you can,

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.140
even if you don't explicitly realize

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.590
that's what you're doing.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.270
Now, Britney is a
celebrity in her own right

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.980
because she's a hard
working triple threat.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:57.930
But if she's out here selling Sketchers,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.800
she's imbuing it with
clout and those sweet,

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.640
sweet kicks are reinforcing
that clout right back at her.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.780
To paraphrase scholar Richard Dyer,

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.670
celebrities are both labor,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.760
ie, varying combinations
of talent, hotness,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.042
work ethic, and luck, and the
thing that labor produces,

00:09:15.042 --> 00:09:17.500
ie, fame and fortune.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.310
So, it follows that when people

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:21.770
with major fan bases endorse a product

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.130
not only does the celebrity
get more attention

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.160
in the public eye,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.500
but the product itself will see a boost

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.230
in profits and prestige.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:32.490
What Babe Ruth did for cola or
Oprah did for Weight Watchers

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.500
or Michael Jordan did for
Nike is something the makers

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.710
of NFTs are ready to
harness for a new century.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:42.960
Now, you've seen Justin Bieber
somehow make Crocs seem cool

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.980
and a water bottle became
spiritually rejuvenating

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.600
because of Jennifer Aniston.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.790
But, something different
happens with crypto and NFTs,

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.890
which, after, all don't tangibly
exist in the real world.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:56.610
Because there's so intangible,

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:01.050
these non fungible tokens
are ironically very fungible

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.770
in terms of what they
can potentially signify.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:06.600
When Paris endorses
them, they're glamorous.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.830
When Spike Lee endorses crypto,
it's righteous and bold.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:11.140
When Gwyneth Paltrow does it,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.500
you're living that crunchy
yet chic holistic lifestyle.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.900
Crypto and, by extension, NFTs
are the perfect empty vessel

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.210
upon which to project a
celebrity's entire affect.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.920
And nowadays there's
an NFT happy celebrity

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.270
for seemingly every demographic.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.290
Even Eric Andre's into it!

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:31.260
Who would've thought absurdist comedians

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.230
even knew about NFTs?

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.130
But, when you're buying an NFT

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.100
to emulate your favorite celebrity,

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.040
it's an even bigger dopamine hit

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.890
than buying those
adorable Spears Sketchers.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.380
You feel closer to them
and, more importantly,

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.090
more similar to them than ever before.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:48.610
Before you only had the pleasure

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:50.900
of consuming like a celebrity.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.130
Now, you have the pleasure of
investing like a celebrity.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:58.530
- What? Here, high-end, VIP, exclusive!

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.460
- That's liable to make you feel richer

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:04.540
and more glamorous than say
drinkin' a Pepsi at a protest.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.397
After all, as Nayer says,

00:11:06.397 --> 00:11:10.220
"Celebrities embody an
abstract desire to achieve,

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.810
to be recognized, to be wealthy.

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.910
Poverty-stricken celebrities
would be hard to find.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.030
Celebrities represent what people aspire

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.100
to be or to possess."

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.060
And, in a world where
financial security is anything

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.220
but easy to come by,

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.770
emulating the investment choices

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.580
of an already wealthy
celebrity might not seem

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:29.689
like the worst option.

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:31.780
Now, we're not grumps.

